 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Reduces the number of MLP evaluations from $O(N)$ per ray (where $N$ is the number of samples) to $O(1)$ per ray for the main radiance field, with only a small network evaluation for view-dependent effects. This significantly speeds up rendering.
-- **Memory Complexity:** Uses a sparse 3D grid and a 3D texture atlas. The sparsity loss helps minimize memory usage for empty regions [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Real-time view synthesis capabilities due to reduced computational cost. Efficient memory usage for large scenes.
-- **Limitations:** May struggle with complex view-dependent effects if the small network is too simple. Requires a baking step during training.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Reduced compared to standard NeRF due to fewer MLP evaluations [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduced VRAM usage via sparse voxels and caching [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Enables real-time view synthesis [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast rendering, memory efficient [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** May lose high-frequency details due to caching and decomposition [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time applications, large scenes [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** High-fidelity offline rendering where detail is critical [S1].
 
 ## Background
-SNeRG was introduced by Hedman et al. in "Baking Neural Radiance Fields for Real-Time View Synthesis" (ICCV 2021) [S1]. It builds on the NeRF framework by addressing its high computational cost through deferred shading and caching techniques.
+SNeRG was introduced by Hedman et al. in "Baking Neural Radiance Fields for Real-Time View Synthesis" (ICCV 2021) [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the original NeRF work by Merity et al. [S1]. The method addresses the high computational cost of NeRF by decomposing the radiance field and using deferred shading [S1].
 
